WEBVTT

00:00:08.030 --> 00:00:12.630
 Welcome to Delicious Revolution, a show about food, culture, and place. I'm Chelsea Wills.

00:00:12.630 --> 00:00:21.310
 In this fifth season, we're speaking with visionary chefs, gardeners, farmers, organizers, artists, and scientists about migrations of all kinds.

00:00:21.310 --> 00:00:31.650
 We'll hear about food and the experience of leaving home and in finding new ones, of decolonizing food traditions and tracing recipes through the movements of diaspora.

00:00:31.650 --> 00:00:35.740
 Delicious Revolution is made by Devon Samson and Chelsea Wills.

00:00:35.740 --> 00:00:40.680
 You can subscribe to the show on Apple Podcasts or wherever you get your podcasts.

00:00:40.680 --> 00:00:48.120
 And you can find all of our episodes along with pictures and more on the website deliciousrevolutionshow.com.

00:00:48.120 --> 00:00:57.520
 Norma Listman is a Mexico City and Oakland-based chef and artist.

00:00:57.520 --> 00:01:04.030
 Her practice is shaped by her heritage and she's most interested in traditional cooking methods and the historical periods of Mexican gastronomy.

00:01:04.030 --> 00:01:09.160
 Norma's passion for the preservation of her culture and her father's lifelong work with maize

00:01:09.160 --> 00:01:13.960
 having ignited her interest for working with native varieties of the crop.

00:01:13.960 --> 00:01:22.660
 She began her career in some of the most prestigious restaurants of the Bay Area, managing the nationally-acclaimed Camino restaurant and long-time Bay Area institution,

00:01:22.660 --> 00:01:27.220
 Bay Wolf restaurant in Oakland, before deciding to follow her passion and become a professional chef.

00:01:27.220 --> 00:01:32.160
 As a food scholar, she teaches Mexican culinary techniques at 18 Reasons in San Francisco.

00:01:32.160 --> 00:01:37.920
 She currently lives in Mexico City and is focused on her research on Mexican native corn and nixtamalization.

00:01:38.360 --> 00:01:47.660
 In this episode, Norma talks with Chelsea about preserving traditional corn varieties in a challenging cultural and economic landscape and on the mestizaje of food traditions in Mexico and California.

00:01:48.570 --> 00:01:51.650
 Welcome to the show.

00:01:51.650 --> 00:01:52.440
 Thank you.

00:01:52.440 --> 00:01:53.520
 It's nice to have you.

00:01:53.520 --> 00:01:55.700
 I'm here with Norma Listman today.

00:01:55.820 --> 00:01:56.780
 She's in Mexico City.

00:01:57.080 --> 00:02:02.080
 And I guess I wanted to just start when I was reading about you and learning about you.

00:02:02.200 --> 00:02:05.360
 It seemed to me like food is really personal to you.

00:02:05.460 --> 00:02:09.800
 Your relationship with cooking and creating starts in a really personal place.

00:02:09.800 --> 00:02:11.000
 Is that true?

00:02:11.490 --> 00:02:12.560
 It is.

00:02:12.560 --> 00:02:23.570
 And it is usually personal in the sense that it's linked to a history of my birthplace, of Mexico.

00:02:24.480 --> 00:02:29.640
 And in that sense, that's how my cooking and my research starts always.

00:02:30.570 --> 00:02:32.630
 So who did you first cook with?

00:02:32.990 --> 00:02:34.460
 What's your first memory of cooking?

00:02:34.910 --> 00:02:37.350
 My first memory of cooking is with grandpa.

00:02:37.350 --> 00:02:47.790
 Like he has this recipe that he invented for the family famous beans that he makes that take over 10 hours to cook.

00:02:48.100 --> 00:02:55.900
 So my first memories is with grandpa, stirring a pot for hours and just talking and being with him.

00:02:56.600 --> 00:02:57.660
 How old do you think you were?

00:02:58.350 --> 00:03:00.410
 Probably around six or seven.

00:03:00.610 --> 00:03:02.690
 I spent a lot of time with my grandfather.

00:03:02.890 --> 00:03:08.540
 He's my favorite person in the world probably and was when I was a little girl.

00:03:08.540 --> 00:03:17.430
 So with him and then not necessarily of cooking, but of food exploration was my grandmother.

00:03:18.190 --> 00:03:24.430
 She and my and her sister, my aunt, who's a matriarch or was a matriarch of the family.

00:03:24.570 --> 00:03:29.290
 I say was because she's dead, but is because she's still very present.

00:03:30.010 --> 00:03:38.410
 And we would they would take a lot of trips around the country that were usually via train or in a bus.

00:03:38.410 --> 00:03:45.850
 They, for the most part, had a religious first purpose that we would go visit a saint or a church.

00:03:46.410 --> 00:03:51.130
 And then from there, it was like almost like get the religious thing done.

00:03:51.350 --> 00:03:56.880
 And then we would go to a park and have picnics, but always would visit the markets.

00:03:57.020 --> 00:04:00.720
 The markets in Mexico are always, as you know, next to a church.

00:04:00.860 --> 00:04:05.480
 We're kind of part of the center plaza and it is where everything happens.

00:04:05.480 --> 00:04:08.160
 So we would go and pay respects to whatever saint.

00:04:08.680 --> 00:04:14.960
 I didn't grow under any particular religion, exposed to many, but, you know, free.

00:04:15.770 --> 00:04:20.230
 And so we would go and do that and then head to the market to eat.

00:04:20.810 --> 00:04:28.930
 And that exposed me from a really early age to the diversity and the wealth that we have in Mexico in terms of food.

00:04:29.070 --> 00:04:30.550
 And it was really exciting.

00:04:30.550 --> 00:04:43.230
 It was always like, oh, now we're going to go to this little town and we're going to eat this type of tamales or this mole or, you know, this soup that they only make in the Sierra.

00:04:43.590 --> 00:04:44.930
 And that was exciting.

00:04:45.130 --> 00:04:52.670
 That sort of instigated my curiosity for Mexican food at a really early age.

00:04:53.440 --> 00:04:55.600
 That sounds like a pretty amazing way to grow up.

00:04:55.640 --> 00:04:56.880
 It sounds like such an adventure.

00:04:57.300 --> 00:04:57.800
 It was.

00:04:57.940 --> 00:05:00.160
 My grandmother's rather adventurous.

00:05:00.600 --> 00:05:00.780
 Yeah.

00:05:01.840 --> 00:05:12.900
 When you traveled with your grandmother, also, it sounds like one of the things that really stood out is this incredible diversity and that every place is not the same.

00:05:13.660 --> 00:05:14.020
 Absolutely.

00:05:15.450 --> 00:05:22.110
 And so, well, I mean, I think a commonly held knowledge in the United States is that Mexican food is Mexican food.

00:05:22.110 --> 00:05:24.650
 And as you're describing it, that's not really the case.

00:05:26.080 --> 00:05:26.360
 No.

00:05:26.480 --> 00:05:28.260
 And this is a really interesting point.

00:05:28.340 --> 00:05:33.060
 And I'm sort of just developing the language around what I'm about to say.

00:05:33.060 --> 00:05:46.590
 But I feel like the U.S., in a way, it's going through their mestizaje as we're speaking, you know, and that's being reflected on the food.

00:05:47.360 --> 00:05:51.180
 Mexico went through this mestizaje 300 years ago.

00:05:51.400 --> 00:06:01.030
 And even before that, we already had a very rich culinary history in Mexico that was regional and that had banquets.

00:06:01.030 --> 00:06:05.490
 And it was very, very sophisticated food culture.

00:06:05.870 --> 00:06:08.670
 There was a lot of knowledge around nutrition.

00:06:09.250 --> 00:06:16.740
 And then the Spanish came and it was a long process of blending.

00:06:16.960 --> 00:06:20.680
 I suppose that at the beginning, it was a lot of, and that I suppose it was like that.

00:06:20.800 --> 00:06:27.460
 There is documentation of people rejecting each other's food and not really understanding it.

00:06:27.460 --> 00:06:36.840
 And then today, it's the result of all of that, of all of the mixing of so many cultures in one.

00:06:37.910 --> 00:06:46.600
 And that is, I think, in the current food discourse, that part around Mexican food, that it's a part that is missing.

00:06:46.860 --> 00:06:51.840
 That it's a part that nobody is really talking about when we talk about Mexican food.

00:06:52.220 --> 00:06:54.780
 Mexican food is not just Mexican food.

00:06:55.100 --> 00:06:56.040
 It's African food.

00:06:56.180 --> 00:06:57.440
 It's European food.

00:06:57.540 --> 00:06:58.720
 It's Asian food.

00:06:59.140 --> 00:07:03.220
 And it is South American and North American in one.

00:07:03.220 --> 00:07:09.830
 Can you talk about some examples of things, of dishes that you think of when you're talking about the mestizaje?

00:07:10.480 --> 00:07:11.160
 Yeah, absolutely.

00:07:11.380 --> 00:07:15.720
 The classic example of one of them is the mole poblano, right?

00:07:15.720 --> 00:07:20.840
 We have the classic pre-Hispanic ingredients.

00:07:21.260 --> 00:07:23.720
 And then we added the bread.

00:07:24.060 --> 00:07:25.320
 We added the nuts.

00:07:25.640 --> 00:07:28.480
 And it's a Baroque dish.

00:07:28.700 --> 00:07:32.400
 You know, it's a great representation of that time.

00:07:33.000 --> 00:07:37.200
 There is a rice, for example.

00:07:37.340 --> 00:07:38.840
 Mexican rice is another one.

00:07:39.380 --> 00:07:41.200
 Rice is not endemic to Mexico.

00:07:42.390 --> 00:07:45.120
 And I mean, I can just go on.

00:07:45.180 --> 00:07:47.060
 There is a famous soup in Nayarit.

00:07:47.240 --> 00:07:49.940
 There is a sopa de jaiba with ginger and chiles.

00:07:50.640 --> 00:07:56.060
 Ginger is also not an ingredient that is from, that is native to the Americas.

00:07:56.060 --> 00:07:59.900
 And yeah, so I find all of this fascinating.

00:08:00.710 --> 00:08:05.070
 So I love this idea that you're talking about, though, like that food history is growing together.

00:08:05.370 --> 00:08:11.400
 Often times is imagined as being this kind of seamless process, right?

00:08:12.240 --> 00:08:14.080
 Seamless is not exactly the right word.

00:08:15.090 --> 00:08:18.210
 Yeah, I kind of understand what you're saying, that it happened naturally.

00:08:18.530 --> 00:08:19.730
 Right, that it happened naturally.

00:08:19.890 --> 00:08:29.490
 But I love this idea that you're talking about where it's kind of like everybody's pushing back on each other all the time or responding to each other.

00:08:30.230 --> 00:08:35.070
 Yeah, and if you think about it, it's a natural human condition that you're trying.

00:08:35.210 --> 00:08:36.610
 It's preservation, right?

00:08:36.700 --> 00:08:45.560
 Imagine you encounter yourself and think about immigrants today and for the past 100 years in the U.S.

00:08:45.560 --> 00:08:48.360
 You're trying to hold on to your identity.

00:08:48.920 --> 00:08:52.740
 And food is the easiest way in which you can do that.

00:08:52.880 --> 00:08:54.200
 You hold on to tradition.

00:08:54.600 --> 00:08:56.460
 You hold on to family.

00:08:56.780 --> 00:08:58.080
 You hold on to your heritage.

00:08:58.520 --> 00:09:02.530
 And you do it in a way that you protect it.

00:09:02.910 --> 00:09:04.650
 You don't want it to mix, you know.

00:09:05.290 --> 00:09:08.130
 But then time pass and generation changes.

00:09:08.710 --> 00:09:13.490
 And, you know, there is, it's like the mixing of people.

00:09:13.770 --> 00:09:17.010
 And with that comes the mixing of food as well.

00:09:17.010 --> 00:09:24.500
 And it's sort of like that's how the new eras of food are born.

00:09:25.220 --> 00:09:41.190
 And I think that the U.S., right now, it's still going in this, like, I see it more how ingredients from one place are being used with a technique from another one, from another country.

00:09:41.470 --> 00:09:44.070
 And it's starting to happen little by little.

00:09:44.150 --> 00:09:45.650
 And it's different from fusion.

00:09:46.150 --> 00:09:54.110
 Fusion had an intellectual process, has an intellectual process rather than a social process, right?

00:09:54.430 --> 00:10:01.430
 Fusion food, it's something, a person comes and says, I'm going to blend the food from this country with the food from this country.

00:10:01.650 --> 00:10:06.370
 And this is going to be the result and whatever, like, really famous in the 90s.

00:10:07.470 --> 00:10:12.010
 But the mestizaje of food, it's different.

00:10:12.010 --> 00:10:14.350
 And it is something new.

00:10:14.950 --> 00:10:22.710
 So I'm just like, it being outside of the U.S., it's also giving me a different perspective from when I was in there.

00:10:22.870 --> 00:10:24.290
 And I see it happening.

00:10:24.290 --> 00:10:29.690
 And I see right now still the resistance to the blending.

00:10:29.690 --> 00:10:31.290
 And I think that it's natural.

00:10:31.290 --> 00:10:35.450
 But I see a little bit of things happening.

00:10:35.450 --> 00:10:44.650
 And I'm really curious to see how and what is the identity of American cuisine.

00:10:44.650 --> 00:10:49.250
 We've already, like, through the history of food, see California cuisine.

00:10:49.430 --> 00:10:51.350
 And California cuisine, it's very European.

00:10:52.350 --> 00:10:55.970
 But it is also sort of like this blending.

00:10:56.900 --> 00:11:00.200
 And then now it's like the new American cuisine, right?

00:11:00.620 --> 00:11:08.830
 It is drawing from what we today consider already American cuisine, which it's, if we think about it, from other places as well.

00:11:09.830 --> 00:11:18.030
 And I'm just really curious to see where we're going to be in 30 years from now in terms of food.

00:11:18.110 --> 00:11:22.950
 And what is that going to speak about today in the U.S.?

00:11:22.950 --> 00:11:23.430
 Absolutely.

00:11:23.730 --> 00:11:27.970
 I think I just interviewed Carla Diaz, who does this Prison Gourmet Project.

00:11:28.480 --> 00:11:29.800
 Are you familiar with her?

00:11:30.120 --> 00:11:31.300
 No, I am not.

00:11:31.300 --> 00:11:33.840
 She works with inmates, too.

00:11:34.240 --> 00:11:37.200
 They send her their recipes and she cooks them.

00:11:38.020 --> 00:11:39.940
 The food comes from commissaries.

00:11:40.400 --> 00:11:50.400
 And, you know, it's like she's making things like she makes orange chicken out of, like, Fritos and strawberry jelly and Cholula and all these things.

00:11:50.520 --> 00:12:05.620
 But it's interesting for me to think about that as you are talking about, you know, this Mestis Saheb idea, because I think, for me, what you're talking about with that is that it comes kind of from the bottom up rather than from the top down, right?

00:12:05.960 --> 00:12:06.280
 Absolutely.

00:12:06.300 --> 00:12:08.820
 So the pushing back is contextual.

00:12:09.120 --> 00:12:09.880
 It's, like, organic.

00:12:10.120 --> 00:12:12.720
 It actually comes from the place it exists in.

00:12:13.580 --> 00:12:14.100
 Exactly.

00:12:14.400 --> 00:12:15.940
 It's a natural human.

00:12:16.990 --> 00:12:23.240
 It's like you try to protect yourself, you know, and you find comfort and you find it.

00:12:23.360 --> 00:12:32.110
 I think that food, it's, like, it's a very accessible way to find that.

00:12:32.110 --> 00:12:43.150
 And then you see people, you know, bringing seeds from their countries and planting their chiles and their vegetables and the things that they can't find or they were not able to find.

00:12:43.310 --> 00:12:52.100
 And all of a sudden, I remember 10 years ago in Oakland, there was, like, there was nowhere I could find Ojasanta.

00:12:52.780 --> 00:12:53.220
 Nowhere.

00:12:53.380 --> 00:12:54.240
 It was impossible.

00:12:54.460 --> 00:12:58.300
 I had one friend in East Oakland that had a little plant.

00:12:58.300 --> 00:13:02.160
 And every once in a while, she would, like, spare some Ojasanta.

00:13:02.490 --> 00:13:05.250
 And now it's available everywhere.

00:13:05.630 --> 00:13:08.450
 You know, it's easy to find and it's not something hard.

00:13:09.330 --> 00:13:18.430
 And that, from a very, like, bottom-up place, it's how food and food systems and food evolves and changes.

00:13:18.670 --> 00:13:21.910
 But it's kind of weird, right?

00:13:22.030 --> 00:13:28.070
 I mean, like, it's like the weirdness is the pushing back and forth.

00:13:28.270 --> 00:13:29.110
 It's not even.

00:13:29.420 --> 00:13:32.020
 That's what I mean in that weirdness, right?

00:13:32.080 --> 00:13:36.640
 Where there's, now there's Ojasanta available, but other things aren't available.

00:13:36.820 --> 00:13:40.130
 So you're still making do with these replacements.

00:13:40.130 --> 00:13:41.810
 And that's how things grow and change.

00:13:41.970 --> 00:13:42.410
 Exactly.

00:13:42.830 --> 00:13:43.270
 Absolutely.

00:13:43.350 --> 00:13:44.810
 But it's kind of awkward, right?

00:13:45.090 --> 00:13:45.250
 So.

00:13:45.830 --> 00:13:46.550
 In what sense?

00:13:46.850 --> 00:13:50.110
 Well, I mean, I feel like kind of awkward and beautiful.

00:13:50.310 --> 00:13:53.850
 Maybe we're kind of segueing into you working with Claudia Fernandez.

00:13:54.190 --> 00:13:58.270
 But it's like this combination of new and old and what's available.

00:13:58.670 --> 00:13:58.930
 Yeah.

00:13:59.210 --> 00:14:00.920
 Well, like making guajalotes.

00:14:01.540 --> 00:14:03.580
 It's bread and bread to me.

00:14:03.700 --> 00:14:04.940
 That's what it always has felt like.

00:14:04.940 --> 00:14:09.300
 A tamale in a sandwich is, like, it's the best thing ever.

00:14:09.300 --> 00:14:12.300
 It's like the ultimate carb load in your life.

00:14:12.420 --> 00:14:14.880
 But it's like a sandwich in a sandwich, right?

00:14:15.540 --> 00:14:16.580
 But that is the feel.

00:14:16.660 --> 00:14:19.560
 If you think about the purpose, it's like food has a purpose.

00:14:19.840 --> 00:14:24.760
 And the food, especially, I think, of people of color has a purpose.

00:14:25.200 --> 00:14:29.370
 Like you don't eat some, you know, you eat to eat to celebrate things.

00:14:29.370 --> 00:14:37.050
 But when you don't have a lot of money, you have to be strategic on how you manage also your calories and your carb intake.

00:14:37.710 --> 00:14:42.530
 And this is something that we know from the Aztecs and how they manage the diet.

00:14:42.930 --> 00:14:47.430
 But if you think about that torta, that torta now it's available and anyone can eat it.

00:14:47.510 --> 00:14:51.470
 And, you know, like we eat it because it's fun and it's, yes, a carb load.

00:14:51.470 --> 00:14:58.390
 But that is what construction workers, what campesinos in the fields eat.

00:14:58.690 --> 00:15:03.110
 And in addition to the tamale being inside the torta, you drink an atole, right?

00:15:03.190 --> 00:15:06.690
 Which it's a corn-based or rice-based drink.

00:15:06.850 --> 00:15:10.060
 And that is like a whole load of carbs.

00:15:10.520 --> 00:15:17.780
 But they are going to be your fuel to go and work in a construction, to go and work in a field, to do all of the labor work.

00:15:17.780 --> 00:15:21.020
 And they're going to hold you over until your next meal.

00:15:22.310 --> 00:15:22.470
 Totally.

00:15:22.810 --> 00:15:28.090
 And it all comes in a package that you can bring with you, right?

00:15:28.130 --> 00:15:28.530
 Exactly.

00:15:28.950 --> 00:15:29.280
 Yeah.

00:15:29.540 --> 00:15:32.300
 And, I mean, it makes sense and it lives in a context.

00:15:32.960 --> 00:15:36.750
 And what a fascinating lens to be looking at things from.

00:15:36.950 --> 00:15:42.280
 Like, what are these contexts and how are they growing and changing, you know?

00:15:42.280 --> 00:15:42.620
 Absolutely.

00:15:43.000 --> 00:15:44.620
 With our world in this moment.

00:15:45.230 --> 00:15:46.810
 And that's what history does.

00:15:46.950 --> 00:15:50.210
 And that's why I am obsessed with food and history.

00:15:50.510 --> 00:15:58.100
 Because you can, through food, decide on what side of the history you want to be, you know?

00:15:58.100 --> 00:16:05.240
 And analyze humankind through it and our evolutions and our trends and stats.

00:16:05.520 --> 00:16:08.180
 And, you know, it's fascinating to me.

00:16:08.560 --> 00:16:10.660
 Well, so right now you're thinking a lot about corn.

00:16:11.040 --> 00:16:11.340
 Yes.

00:16:11.440 --> 00:16:15.940
 What are you thinking about corn as a deep subject to get into?

00:16:16.360 --> 00:16:17.160
 I know.

00:16:17.420 --> 00:16:17.820
 Exactly.

00:16:17.980 --> 00:16:19.700
 It is a really deep subject.

00:16:19.940 --> 00:16:24.660
 And, I mean, to be honest, the more it sounds cliche, but the more I know, the less I know

00:16:24.660 --> 00:16:25.830
 kind of thing.

00:16:26.290 --> 00:16:30.090
 I did a lot of work around the Californios and around General Vallejo.

00:16:30.630 --> 00:16:33.530
 And I'm going to say this phrase because I love it and I think it's beautiful.

00:16:33.530 --> 00:16:35.690
 And it's what happened to me.

00:16:35.690 --> 00:16:44.570
 And he said, as their bear revolt was coming through Texas and coming to California for the

00:16:44.570 --> 00:16:49.780
 takeover, he said, the future is for those who can hear it coming.

00:16:51.890 --> 00:16:53.530
 And that happened to me with corn.

00:16:53.710 --> 00:17:01.040
 All of a sudden, I'm in the US and there's all this buzz around Mexican native corn.

00:17:01.480 --> 00:17:07.440
 About five years ago, you would say the word corn and people right away would jump and say,

00:17:07.540 --> 00:17:07.960
 Monsanto.

00:17:08.420 --> 00:17:14.000
 It's like corn is a devil and all of the things and corn had been demonized.

00:17:14.880 --> 00:17:22.290
 But from a couple of years ago forward, people are just talking about Mexican native corn.

00:17:22.910 --> 00:17:27.710
 And there's a lot of interest and a lot of interest around that and nixtamalization.

00:17:27.710 --> 00:17:38.590
 And the language that I started to hear was very capitalist and very like corn is a new crop.

00:17:38.910 --> 00:17:42.770
 Pairing, making parallels between the corn industry and the coffee industry.

00:17:43.330 --> 00:17:47.860
 But I just had this feeling that I needed to be here.

00:17:47.980 --> 00:17:51.740
 And first it was like, OK, I'm going to start working with corn and like bringing good corn

00:17:51.740 --> 00:17:53.640
 to the US and doing all of this.

00:17:53.740 --> 00:17:57.580
 And then I was, wait a minute, I have the opportunity to be in Mexico.

00:17:57.960 --> 00:18:03.190
 In the ground, running, my dad has been working with corn for many years.

00:18:03.190 --> 00:18:06.840
 And I was just like, what am I doing?

00:18:07.320 --> 00:18:12.640
 And it was one of the biggest forces for me to move back to Mexico.

00:18:13.020 --> 00:18:21.590
 Hearing everything that was happening in the US and how it potentially, the industry can go sour

00:18:21.590 --> 00:18:25.650
 if there's not enough people in the ground doing the good work.

00:18:26.230 --> 00:18:30.670
 And I came to Mexico and I started meeting people.

00:18:30.870 --> 00:18:34.160
 I started going to corn fairs, congresses.

00:18:34.360 --> 00:18:43.220
 And there are actually a lot of people working and fighting to preserve our native Mexican corn.

00:18:43.220 --> 00:18:50.720
 So it's, there is a lot of work to be done, but it is, I feel very fortunate and privileged

00:18:50.720 --> 00:18:58.320
 to be at this moment in time in Mexico, witnessing what is happening and being able to be a part of it.

00:18:58.600 --> 00:19:02.560
 So for people who don't know, could you explain what nixtamalization is?

00:19:04.400 --> 00:19:07.890
 Alcoholization is the process.

00:19:07.890 --> 00:19:15.250
 It's a Mesoamerican process that the Aztecs and the Malians used, in which you soak your corn,

00:19:15.350 --> 00:19:17.430
 your dry corn, in an alkaline solution.

00:19:18.270 --> 00:19:27.140
 Traditionally, it was made with ash or limestone produced from shells a lot of the times.

00:19:27.520 --> 00:19:34.300
 And what that alkaline solution does is changes the make of the grain so that it could release

00:19:34.300 --> 00:19:44.450
 proteins and makes it digestible for humans and also changes the flavor profile and enhances

00:19:44.450 --> 00:19:45.430
 the flavor profile.

00:19:45.430 --> 00:19:59.380
 So what happens is that you boil the corn with this lime, then you let it rest for anywhere

00:19:59.380 --> 00:20:05.700
 between eight to 14 hours, and then you wash it off and you grind your corn and you have

00:20:05.700 --> 00:20:08.380
 what it's called nixtamalized corn.

00:20:08.380 --> 00:20:15.780
 Your next amount is the corn when it's actually like soaked and has gone through this process.

00:20:16.260 --> 00:20:19.640
 And it happens every day, all the time, right?

00:20:20.300 --> 00:20:22.100
 Yep, it happens every day, all the time.

00:20:22.500 --> 00:20:31.440
 I mean, the work that I am focusing on right now is research, but also how can we, because

00:20:31.440 --> 00:20:35.300
 Mexico City does not eat good tortillas anymore.

00:20:35.800 --> 00:20:37.760
 Everybody uses bad corn.

00:20:37.760 --> 00:20:40.000
 And there's also good corn and bad corn, right?

00:20:40.430 --> 00:20:47.780
 That the nixtamal process happens every day, yet it doesn't guarantee a good product if

00:20:47.780 --> 00:20:49.620
 you're not using good corn.

00:20:49.780 --> 00:20:51.760
 So what's a good corn and what's a bad corn?

00:20:52.700 --> 00:20:58.760
 I would say a bad corn would be genetically modified corn for people, and a good corn would

00:20:58.760 --> 00:21:04.210
 be one of our native corns, non-genetically modified corn.

00:21:04.790 --> 00:21:11.970
 What's happening is that in Mexico, it's also importing a lot of corn from the U.S., supposedly

00:21:11.970 --> 00:21:13.830
 mainly for animal feed.

00:21:14.370 --> 00:21:22.930
 But it also filters and trickles into the corn that we are eating, and it is contaminating

00:21:22.930 --> 00:21:24.270
 Mexican corn.

00:21:24.270 --> 00:21:32.460
 So the quality, it's like, you know, night and day, and the results of what you get in

00:21:32.460 --> 00:21:34.200
 a tortilla are very different.

00:21:34.320 --> 00:21:41.680
 Though, what's interesting is that people in Mexico City are used to the taste of minza

00:21:41.680 --> 00:21:43.560
 and masteca already.

00:21:43.720 --> 00:21:48.960
 So the question is like, how to, like, I think that there's a lot of education that needs

00:21:48.960 --> 00:21:55.000
 to happen, and it first needs to happen in Mexico before it goes anywhere, you know?

00:21:55.220 --> 00:21:56.780
 And that is sort of the fear.

00:21:57.160 --> 00:22:04.080
 It's like this, from being in both countries, like, I see the two realities sort of fighting,

00:22:04.260 --> 00:22:11.130
 you know, like the U.S. market wanting, like, the new, you know, beautiful thing, and then

00:22:11.130 --> 00:22:14.000
 Mexico, it's like, well, do we have enough?

00:22:14.340 --> 00:22:21.720
 The fight here is producing enough corn for the national consumption, and then exporting

00:22:21.720 --> 00:22:24.250
 the rest of that beautiful corn, you know?

00:22:24.310 --> 00:22:26.670
 And it's a very delicate subject.

00:22:27.270 --> 00:22:31.900
 It's not, there's no easy answer, and I don't think that there is a straight answer.

00:22:31.900 --> 00:22:38.120
 And I think that there are a lot of people doing work and trying to, you know, trying

00:22:38.120 --> 00:22:43.160
 to get to how do we do it in a way that it's good for everyone.

00:22:43.480 --> 00:22:44.620
 And so what do you think?

00:22:44.740 --> 00:22:45.800
 How do you think that's done?

00:22:46.540 --> 00:22:53.280
 I think that the country is figuring it out, and I think that it's a hard one because how

00:22:53.280 --> 00:22:59.080
 do you, for example, tell a farmer with only two acres that it's producing the weirdest

00:22:59.080 --> 00:23:07.080
 corn, and all of a sudden, like a fancy restaurant chain in the U.S. comes and wants it, you know?

00:23:07.300 --> 00:23:13.940
 And it's like, that means money, and it's money that people that don't have much of it want,

00:23:14.060 --> 00:23:18.780
 so they will sell their production for that money and would stop eating that corn.

00:23:18.960 --> 00:23:23.910
 Like, there are traditions that are stopped, and there is a loss of culture.

00:23:24.470 --> 00:23:26.870
 So I don't have an answer for you.

00:23:27.090 --> 00:23:31.370
 That is the work that is being done right now, and it's being tried.

00:23:31.550 --> 00:23:38.160
 Like, as we're speaking, we're trying to figure it out because this interest of corn from the U.S.,

00:23:38.160 --> 00:23:39.080
 it's sort of new.

00:23:39.240 --> 00:23:42.440
 So all of this is new, and I don't know.

00:23:42.440 --> 00:23:50.220
 I mean, part of me just wants to say, U.S., like, can you just, like, wait a moment and not, you know,

00:23:50.320 --> 00:23:52.560
 not be up in our business for a while?

00:23:52.820 --> 00:23:54.340
 But that's not going to happen.

00:23:55.030 --> 00:23:59.040
 So to tell you the truth, I don't have an answer, and that's why I'm here.

00:23:59.160 --> 00:23:59.800
 I'm observing.

00:24:00.240 --> 00:24:01.460
 I'm talking to people.

00:24:02.020 --> 00:24:04.760
 I am, you know, I'm talking to scientists.

00:24:04.760 --> 00:24:06.760
 I'm talking to farmers.

00:24:07.140 --> 00:24:10.500
 I am talking to people who are exporting corn from Mexico.

00:24:10.500 --> 00:24:12.680
 I am talking to the intermediaries.

00:24:12.860 --> 00:24:15.860
 I'm talking to the people, you know, cooking it here.

00:24:16.340 --> 00:24:19.140
 And, yeah, we are in the thick of it.

00:24:19.500 --> 00:24:21.080
 Well, a few things occurred to me.

00:24:21.180 --> 00:24:24.800
 One is that when I was living in Yucatan and working with campesinos there,

00:24:25.170 --> 00:24:28.220
 everybody had a story about the things that were in Maseca.

00:24:28.780 --> 00:24:30.940
 And it was amazing.

00:24:30.940 --> 00:24:35.440
 I wish I had recorded all of them because, you know, everybody would tell me, like,

00:24:35.520 --> 00:24:40.780
 they found ground-up rats in Maseca and they found, you know, all of these disgusting things,

00:24:40.860 --> 00:24:43.280
 which, who knows, it's really ground-up.

00:24:43.360 --> 00:24:45.270
 It's a little hard to tell in flour.

00:24:45.430 --> 00:24:45.610
 Right.

00:24:45.870 --> 00:24:51.350
 But I love that idea of the imagination of, like, what goes into industrially produced flour.

00:24:51.690 --> 00:24:52.090
 Correct.

00:24:52.410 --> 00:24:54.750
 Because it's probably all true and none of it's true.

00:24:54.990 --> 00:25:00.110
 Like, it's this real and imagined thing that's so ripe for everything.

00:25:00.110 --> 00:25:04.490
 But the other is that I became a real snob about tortillas.

00:25:04.970 --> 00:25:05.090
 Yeah.

00:25:05.270 --> 00:25:11.070
 And I really stopped eating tortillas most places except my friends' houses who were farmers.

00:25:11.490 --> 00:25:11.710
 Yeah.

00:25:12.070 --> 00:25:14.150
 Because you couldn't get a good tortilla.

00:25:14.430 --> 00:25:18.190
 And then I went to Mexico City and everybody was trying to feed me tortillas

00:25:18.190 --> 00:25:20.870
 and I couldn't get a good tortilla, like, in the whole city.

00:25:20.990 --> 00:25:21.430
 Not true.

00:25:21.570 --> 00:25:22.670
 But most places.

00:25:22.850 --> 00:25:25.470
 I became very choosy about where I was eating tortillas.

00:25:25.870 --> 00:25:26.150
 Yeah.

00:25:26.150 --> 00:25:32.250
 And it blew my mind because the other part about Mexico City was the produce that was

00:25:32.250 --> 00:25:34.630
 available was so much more abundant.

00:25:34.630 --> 00:25:38.010
 Well, really, almost than anywhere else in the world that I've ever been.

00:25:38.130 --> 00:25:46.490
 But definitely than these small or mid-sized agricultural towns in the countryside in Mexico.

00:25:47.120 --> 00:25:47.670
 Right.

00:25:47.730 --> 00:25:52.240
 So there was this paradox that was going on that felt really interesting.

00:25:52.480 --> 00:25:57.400
 And so I went back to Yucatan and I started asking people about what's the deal, you know?

00:25:57.880 --> 00:26:03.470
 And they said, I mean, people chose not to sell the good food they grew, right?

00:26:03.550 --> 00:26:03.810
 Right.

00:26:03.970 --> 00:26:04.750
 In large part.

00:26:04.890 --> 00:26:10.370
 So it was totally invisible to a global economy, which I felt like was such an interesting,

00:26:11.110 --> 00:26:13.370
 it felt very obvious because everyone does that, right?

00:26:13.450 --> 00:26:15.130
 The best food is the food from your garden.

00:26:15.530 --> 00:26:15.830
 Absolutely.

00:26:16.490 --> 00:26:21.400
 But from like the outside perspective of someone who cooks and eats and thinks about food as

00:26:21.400 --> 00:26:26.430
 a public thing, also, it gets so complicated, right?

00:26:26.490 --> 00:26:32.390
 In terms of just like what you're saying, where is the line drawn, I guess, in terms of food

00:26:32.390 --> 00:26:33.710
 sovereignty, right?

00:26:33.790 --> 00:26:36.220
 That is what you're talking about in a certain sense.

00:26:36.930 --> 00:26:37.290
 Absolutely.

00:26:37.870 --> 00:26:38.310
 Absolutely.

00:26:38.310 --> 00:26:41.270
 And I think that is something that it's being figured out.

00:26:41.350 --> 00:26:47.990
 And you see the abundance in the markets, you go to La Merced, but all of that is industrialized

00:26:47.990 --> 00:26:49.450
 agriculture, you know?

00:26:49.570 --> 00:26:49.970
 Absolutely.

00:26:50.550 --> 00:26:55.490
 And so I think that the milpa needs to come back, you know?

00:26:55.590 --> 00:27:01.330
 We are losing some of our land, for example, in the state of Michoacan to China, coming to

00:27:01.330 --> 00:27:11.740
 Mexico and like wiping milpas to do monoculture and plant avocado trees, you know, citrus trees.

00:27:11.770 --> 00:27:17.270
 And I think that a lot of the efforts and a lot of what we're talking, when we talk about

00:27:17.270 --> 00:27:23.550
 Mexican food systems, it's about recovering the milpa because the milpa is a center, basically,

00:27:24.010 --> 00:27:25.930
 of culture.

00:27:26.190 --> 00:27:27.270
 It's where everything grows.

00:27:27.510 --> 00:27:36.580
 It's a lot of the ceremonies and everything that happens socially, it's dictated by the

00:27:36.580 --> 00:27:36.880
 milpa.

00:27:36.880 --> 00:27:44.310
 So within that wealth of options that we have in terms of produce, like one has to be

00:27:44.310 --> 00:27:45.690
 more critical as well.

00:27:45.850 --> 00:27:49.530
 And it's like, you know, I really don't care if it's organic or not.

00:27:49.570 --> 00:27:51.470
 Like, that's not what I'm talking about.

00:27:51.770 --> 00:27:54.970
 But it is more like, who is growing it?

00:27:55.050 --> 00:27:56.250
 How is it being grown?

00:27:56.390 --> 00:27:59.550
 Like, I much rather go to my little market in Texcoco.

00:27:59.750 --> 00:28:06.170
 I grew up in Texcoco, 45 minutes away from Mexico City, and buy from Marmar Chanta what

00:28:06.170 --> 00:28:10.290
 she's growing in her milpa, you know, whatever it's available.

00:28:10.670 --> 00:28:17.100
 I go to the market, and like when I just moved here a few months ago, I was going to the Tuesday

00:28:17.100 --> 00:28:23.140
 market in Condesa, but then soon realized that it's rather disappointing that there are two

00:28:23.140 --> 00:28:31.490
 good vendors, one lady that is deaf, that has like a teeny tiny table with things, and another

00:28:31.490 --> 00:28:32.910
 person, you know.

00:28:33.290 --> 00:28:38.620
 And then there is, of course, Mercado Cien, which is the organic market that it's, I love

00:28:38.620 --> 00:28:42.200
 it, but it's not everybody can pay the prices for that market.

00:28:42.560 --> 00:28:50.290
 So there is a lot of layers to it, like there are in anywhere that you're talking about food

00:28:50.290 --> 00:28:51.230
 systems, right?

00:28:51.390 --> 00:28:54.420
 So how do you navigate that in a city that size?

00:28:54.740 --> 00:29:00.930
 It's so interesting for me, because you're coming back, you have old and new eyes, right?

00:29:01.030 --> 00:29:01.490
 Exactly.

00:29:02.690 --> 00:29:04.710
 Well, I mean, what an interesting perspective.

00:29:05.230 --> 00:29:06.630
 How do I navigate it?

00:29:06.730 --> 00:29:10.780
 I am, I feel very new, you know, like you just nailed it.

00:29:10.840 --> 00:29:15.960
 Like, I think that the, it's my country, it's my city, it's really familiar.

00:29:16.120 --> 00:29:22.930
 I've been coming, like, sometimes five times a year for the last 17 years, but it's different

00:29:22.930 --> 00:29:26.310
 when you don't leave in a city and you don't have those connections.

00:29:26.310 --> 00:29:30.210
 I have those connections in Oakland with my farmers and that, and I'm just making those.

00:29:30.210 --> 00:29:35.790
 I am just going to what I knew, which is the market in my hometown.

00:29:37.230 --> 00:29:41.750
 There are little trotillerias popping out and I'm starting to make those relationships.

00:29:41.950 --> 00:29:43.670
 I'm growing things in my garden.

00:29:44.130 --> 00:29:50.930
 I am getting the basket from Yolkan, you know, or when I do events, I try to buy from them.

00:29:51.390 --> 00:29:56.030
 Yolkan is a farm that is in the Chinampas of Xochimilco.

00:29:56.470 --> 00:30:03.750
 And that has been doing a lot of work with the farmers from Xochimilco to preserve traditional

00:30:03.750 --> 00:30:10.450
 techniques of farming, which it's the Chinampas, which are these mud beds that are constructed

00:30:10.450 --> 00:30:18.450
 in the, what is left of the Lake of the Skoko, and the plants just take whatever water they

00:30:18.450 --> 00:30:22.090
 need from their root system because they're like on top of the canals.

00:30:22.170 --> 00:30:27.270
 He's doing beautiful work there, so I try to buy from there as much as possible.

00:30:27.550 --> 00:30:33.540
 And then there is a, you know, the Milpalta market and you start meeting people, but it

00:30:33.540 --> 00:30:34.080
 takes time.

00:30:34.160 --> 00:30:40.030
 And I realized that it's going to take a few years for me to be able to navigate myself

00:30:40.030 --> 00:30:42.170
 through Mexico and the food system again.

00:30:42.820 --> 00:30:45.400
 What is the conversation that you're a part of there?

00:30:45.580 --> 00:30:46.960
 Do you feel like there's some buzz?

00:30:47.320 --> 00:30:49.160
 Who are you in conversation with about this?

00:30:49.160 --> 00:30:56.340
 I'm, I'm, I'm in conversation with Francisco Muzi with Sofia Casarin.

00:30:56.500 --> 00:31:00.940
 They have a really good, they're starting a corn business that I'm not going to say too

00:31:00.940 --> 00:31:01.500
 much about.

00:31:01.620 --> 00:31:09.040
 I talked to Nikki, but I am very silent at the moment because what's happening to me, it's

00:31:09.040 --> 00:31:17.840
 like, I, you cannot apply the same ideologies that we have in the U S to Mexico.

00:31:17.840 --> 00:31:24.410
 And I am in a process of observation and just like wide eyed.

00:31:24.610 --> 00:31:33.200
 And I feel like my brain, it's molding again, you know, like you cannot apply the same ideologies

00:31:33.200 --> 00:31:35.540
 to one country, to another.

00:31:35.540 --> 00:31:38.580
 Like it's everything, it's different though.

00:31:38.580 --> 00:31:46.620
 We have a lot of similarities, there are enormous differences and I am just learning those differences.

00:31:46.620 --> 00:31:51.850
 And my brain is just like, I feel that it's like dough right now.

00:31:51.930 --> 00:31:56.580
 It's just like, you know, being challenged and amazed.

00:31:56.580 --> 00:32:01.080
 Is this the next tamalization of Norma right now?

00:32:02.530 --> 00:32:04.680
 Yeah, probably.

00:32:05.280 --> 00:32:06.420
 Sorry, it was too good.

00:32:06.500 --> 00:32:07.040
 I had to.

00:32:09.080 --> 00:32:13.770
 I'm like, well, am I going to become a tamal or a tortilla, right?

00:32:13.870 --> 00:32:14.410
 Who knows?

00:32:14.470 --> 00:32:15.390
 You could be anything.

00:32:16.070 --> 00:32:16.630
 Exactly.

00:32:18.230 --> 00:32:25.400
 I think something else unique about how you're thinking about this is you are still connected.

00:32:25.960 --> 00:32:26.790
 I mean, okay.

00:32:26.870 --> 00:32:32.570
 So one of the assumptions I think that people have about this idea of a return to a traditional

00:32:32.570 --> 00:32:37.890
 diet in some ways, or pre-colonial food is that it's not connected to the rest of the world.

00:32:38.290 --> 00:32:40.770
 And obviously that's not true.

00:32:40.950 --> 00:32:42.370
 And it never has been true.

00:32:42.730 --> 00:32:46.420
 But for you, your work exists in so many places.

00:32:46.970 --> 00:32:47.070
 Yeah.

00:32:47.270 --> 00:32:51.970
 You, you're investigating these same things in the United States and in Mexico.

00:32:51.970 --> 00:32:57.040
 And, you know, it sounds like you travel and do that other places you investigated in art

00:32:57.040 --> 00:32:59.580
 museums, you investigated in restaurants.

00:33:00.060 --> 00:33:01.130
 Because we're connected.

00:33:01.390 --> 00:33:02.850
 Food connects us.

00:33:02.970 --> 00:33:11.600
 And I mean, the one thing that I do think about the, that it's a very romantic thought

00:33:11.600 --> 00:33:20.170
 in terms of, you know, there's a lot of, uh, the discourse around colonization and decolonization,

00:33:20.170 --> 00:33:27.400
 um, currently in the food world and in, in like social discourse.

00:33:27.650 --> 00:33:35.580
 And with saying that with that return to the pre-Hispanic diet, the beauty about nyxamalization

00:33:35.580 --> 00:33:37.500
 is that it's not a return.

00:33:38.020 --> 00:33:46.100
 Like it is a one technique in food in the world that has not been colonized.

00:33:46.700 --> 00:33:51.660
 We eat tortillas today the same way that Mesoamericans did.

00:33:52.150 --> 00:33:55.730
 And that to me is fascinating and it's beautiful.

00:33:55.730 --> 00:34:01.750
 And sometimes I just want a cry of joy that that exists, you know?

00:34:02.910 --> 00:34:10.170
 Well, and I think for me that feels so exciting and so rich because these ideas of authenticity

00:34:10.170 --> 00:34:14.640
 or purity, they're kind of turned on their head, right?

00:34:15.160 --> 00:34:18.420
 Like making tortillas happens every day, all the time.

00:34:18.990 --> 00:34:21.510
 It is this, it's an enduring practice, right?

00:34:21.610 --> 00:34:27.670
 It's a dynamic point rather than a static reference that we're looking towards.

00:34:27.670 --> 00:34:32.450
 And so maybe as we're getting towards the end of this, talk a little bit about what's coming

00:34:32.450 --> 00:34:38.020
 up for you and maybe talk about some of your work with Saqib and what you guys are thinking

00:34:38.020 --> 00:34:39.100
 about with these meals.

00:34:40.990 --> 00:34:51.050
 Well, uh, that started out of Nayarit and like, uh, realizing the connection between our homelands,

00:34:51.170 --> 00:34:51.390
 right?

00:34:51.510 --> 00:34:55.970
 And, uh, the connection around ingredients and technique.

00:34:55.970 --> 00:35:00.910
 And most of our work, we have this project called Masala y Maíz.

00:35:00.910 --> 00:35:08.790
 And most of our work researches the movement of ingredients between Mexico and India.

00:35:09.750 --> 00:35:18.840
 And that is, there is so much, and it is such a wealthy field of study that we're getting

00:35:18.840 --> 00:35:19.620
 ourselves into.

00:35:19.740 --> 00:35:24.580
 And also the more we are here and talk to people, the more we realize that are other people already

00:35:24.580 --> 00:35:25.520
 doing this research.

00:35:25.520 --> 00:35:32.910
 And it's fascinating, you know, that we can have and share those experiences with, uh, people

00:35:32.910 --> 00:35:39.350
 and open their eyes about what they're eating today and how it came about to the table.

00:35:39.670 --> 00:35:45.970
 Currently, we just finished a series of dinners that, um, we did here in Mexico City, private

00:35:45.970 --> 00:35:46.430
 dinners.

00:35:46.650 --> 00:35:48.630
 And we are getting ready.

00:35:48.770 --> 00:35:53.410
 There's a really beautiful show at Museo Tamayo by Claudia Fernandez.

00:35:53.610 --> 00:35:54.490
 It's called Ceremonia.

00:35:55.080 --> 00:36:03.320
 And it's basically an investigation of, uh, Mexican craft and utilitary objects.

00:36:03.320 --> 00:36:05.920
 It's her personal collection and somebody else's collection.

00:36:05.920 --> 00:36:13.260
 And she has a map of crafts in Mexico that it's not a geosocial map, but it's, you know,

00:36:13.260 --> 00:36:20.580
 it's not divided, uh, geosocially, but it's just like where the crafts are situated in, in

00:36:20.580 --> 00:36:21.020
 the country.

00:36:21.140 --> 00:36:27.240
 So for this one, our exploration and our research is going to be around shared techniques.

00:36:27.580 --> 00:36:33.460
 And, um, he's coming here next week and we're going to spend a bunch of time with Claudia at

00:36:33.460 --> 00:36:41.080
 the museum, at the show, just exploring all of the different utensils and vajillas and plates

00:36:41.080 --> 00:36:43.480
 and ovens that she has at the show.

00:36:43.600 --> 00:36:44.880
 She even has breads.

00:36:45.620 --> 00:36:50.660
 And from there, we're going to create a meal in July at the museum.

00:36:51.390 --> 00:36:52.210
 That sounds so fun.

00:36:53.150 --> 00:36:54.470
 What a fun way to think through it.

00:36:54.750 --> 00:36:55.030
 Yeah.

00:36:55.390 --> 00:36:55.630
 Yeah.

00:36:55.670 --> 00:36:56.290
 It's exciting.

00:36:56.450 --> 00:37:01.730
 And that's, I mean, the, the beautiful thing about being back in Mexico is that there is

00:37:01.730 --> 00:37:05.490
 a very cosmopolitan thought process.

00:37:05.490 --> 00:37:13.110
 And I think that the art world and the museums are also very evolved and beautiful and, and

00:37:13.110 --> 00:37:19.610
 part of the everyday, you know, I have so many friends who are artists and work in museums

00:37:19.610 --> 00:37:21.010
 and do things and galleries.

00:37:21.010 --> 00:37:22.170
 And it's easy.

00:37:22.410 --> 00:37:26.770
 It's easy to, to have this conversation, to get in and do this, this project.

00:37:26.830 --> 00:37:27.710
 It's really exciting.

00:37:28.180 --> 00:37:28.300
 Yeah.

00:37:28.340 --> 00:37:29.260
 It sounds like it.

00:37:29.720 --> 00:37:36.540
 So maybe just to wrap up, why do you think that you are the right person to, to have this

00:37:36.540 --> 00:37:37.120
 conversation?

00:37:37.120 --> 00:37:40.920
 Like what is uniquely important about your voice in this?

00:37:40.920 --> 00:37:48.630
 I mean, I don't, I think that thinking that I am the right person would be presumptuous

00:37:48.630 --> 00:37:53.790
 other than to say that I am the right person because I am doing the research, you know?

00:37:54.110 --> 00:37:58.720
 Like, I don't, I, I don't know how to answer that question.

00:37:58.960 --> 00:38:05.710
 Like I, I, the work that I do comes from my heart and I don't think that I'm doing it

00:38:05.710 --> 00:38:08.250
 because I think, okay, I'm like the right person to do this.

00:38:08.330 --> 00:38:15.390
 I just do it because I have no option because this is what I have to do with my life.

00:38:15.470 --> 00:38:17.890
 You know, this is what I want to be doing.

00:38:18.110 --> 00:38:21.370
 And yeah, I have no option.

00:38:22.070 --> 00:38:22.910
 Well, I love that.

00:38:22.950 --> 00:38:24.550
 And I didn't mean to put you on the spot.

00:38:24.550 --> 00:38:28.250
 I just feel, I just feel struck by your curiosity with it.

00:38:28.410 --> 00:38:33.450
 And the, the back and forth seems so poignant to me of, of the change in perspective.

00:38:33.450 --> 00:38:39.550
 So, um, yeah, how do we follow along with you and know what your upcoming projects are?

00:38:39.930 --> 00:38:41.490
 You can follow my website.

00:38:41.650 --> 00:38:50.650
 It's normalistman.com and I post, um, upcoming events and some of my writing and thoughts are

00:38:50.650 --> 00:38:53.950
 there and I use Instagram a lot.

00:38:54.400 --> 00:38:55.400
 So it's the same thing.

00:38:55.480 --> 00:39:02.900
 My handle is normalistman and yeah, I will always be posting what I'm doing in either one

00:39:02.900 --> 00:39:03.620
 of those two.

00:39:04.240 --> 00:39:04.600
 Perfect.

00:39:05.160 --> 00:39:05.750
 Okay.

